+# The September teleconference inventory request and April follow-up
+
+November 9, 2026 Eastern. TEL04 / AUD016-A1 / TC05–TC06. A second copy of the September 3, 2003 teleconference request confirms the requested deliverable was an inventory. A separate April 2004 exchange documents a reported response and staff concerns about its coverage. Two same-folder logs help distinguish other occurrences of the time and device labels from the Flight 77 relay. [Exact archive objects and locators](SOURCE-PINS.json) · [Commission collection finding aid](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/finding-aid.pdf)
+
+## Correction to the September request description
+
+The preceding report said the cover requested that agencies including DOJ **“provide responsive transcripts or other documents by September 17, 2003.”** The source instead asks the agencies to provide **“an inventory of tapes, transcripts, or documents”** by that deadline. This corrects the description of the deliverable. The earlier report and both source copies are retained. The body identifies the material to inventory and defines the scope as **08:30 to 12:00 EDT on September 11, 2001**. [Earlier wording](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/3490ed02f6516693d2a06a5559de9460722cc258/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-doj-teleconference-and-watchlog-route-2026-11-09/README.md) · [Archive ZIP, DM Box 8 item 515 and Team 7 Box 20 cover](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+The two copies match on six checked fields: the cover date, subject, DOJ listing, response deadline, inventory language and time window. Distinct archive members and extraction locators are preserved. The comparison supplies evidence of repeated copies of the request wording. The sending transaction, agency acknowledgment, inventories actually returned and any later production of underlying records remain open.
+
+## What staff reported in April
+
+DM Box 8 **item 503** reproduces an April 18, 2004 reply over an April 16 message. The April 16 message questions the value of pursuing operations-center logs, tapes and transcripts if the primary aim was to recover the NOIWON conference recording or transcript. The April 18 reply describes a broader scope, with two categories under discussion: day-of-attack logs and morning teleconference materials. It reports a response that **“all responsive material had been produced.”** The writer separately states that, **“to my knowledge,”** nothing concerning the day itself had been produced by the FBI or CIA. The reply calls the reported response vague, notes a lack of certification, and identifies a wording concern in the earlier request, **“describe”** versus **“related to.”** These are attributed statements and a proposed scope change. The actual response email, its basis and the revised requests as sent remain open. [Archive ZIP, item 503 p. 1, lines 15–29 and 46–55](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+The April reply names the White House secure video teleconference and FAA hijack conference in explaining why it sought more records. Its reference to a September request is consistent with the subject and timing of the two September 3 copies. The reviewed records lack an attachment ID or dispatch join establishing the exact circulated version. The reply concerns FBI and CIA production as understood by the writer. The response to the DOJ portion and custody of its original Command Center Watch Officer Log remain separate. [Item 503, p. 1 lines 18–34](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip) · [Staff report note 268](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=95)
+
+## Nearby logs contain separate observations
+
+- **ACI Watch Log, item 056, p. 1.** The text and page face give **0933** in this separate log. The nearby entry fields provide no join to the Team 8 **09:45** Flight 77 relay. The text on p. 3 labels this packet **ACI Watch Log**. Co-location in Box 17 supplies a finding-aid relationship.
+- **Security Operation Log, item 055, p. 2.** A separate **10:06** log-time field precedes a subject identifying a **UA93** 911 report, with **cell phone** in the body. This is a different flight and a reported device description. It supplies neither a Flight 77 match nor an independent measurement of the UA93 call time or device. [Archive ZIP, Team 8 Box 17 items 055 and 056](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+## Scope and next records
+
+Four mirror ZIP members were acquired and validated by byte range, size, CRC and SHA-256. All **12 physical pages** were screened with the PDF text layers. Selected fields were checked in narrow renders. Poppler reports zero embedded files in each new PDF. The reports preserve documentary and extract-level observations. No call routing, event identity, clock calibration or agency completeness was independently established.
+
+Seek the September inventories and the response email described in April, the final revised request and agency returns, then join any enclosure to its recipient's intake or on-site review record. Seek the full-day DOJ Watch Officer Log named by staff, the source system and native entry behind the Flight 77 relay, and the relevant carrier/operator and receiving-office legs. The corrected FAA answer and signed disposition remain separate open records.
